The taxon registry is shared across projects in the NAHPU database. Register a
taxon manually, import a spreadsheet or delimited file, or scan a NAHPU taxon
QR code.

Importing taxa adds registry entries only; it does not add a new specimen
catalog format. Contributors implementing a new taxon group should follow
[Adding a taxon group](../../contributing/code/adding-a-taxon-group).

## Register a new taxon

A registry entry does not have to be a species. `Taxon rank` {% ref id="F" /%}
sets the rank the record represents (`Class`, `Order`, `Family`, `Genus`,
`Species`, or `Subspecies`), and the form displays name fields down to that
rank. Choosing `Family`, for example, shows `Kingdom`, `Phylum`, `Class`,
`Order`, and `Family` without lower ranks—useful for material not yet
identified to species.

### Required fields

- **`Taxon rank`**: the rank this record represents.
- **Target rank name**: the field matching that rank (`Family` for a
  family-rank record, `Specific epithet` for a species, `Subspecific epithet`
  for a subspecies, and so on). NAHPU will not save until both are filled in.

### Classification fields

- **`Kingdom`** and **`Phylum`**: always available for any rank.
- **`Class`**, **`Order`**, **`Family`**, **`Genus`**, **`Specific epithet`**,
  and **`Subspecific epithet`**: shown down to the selected rank. Epithets are
  stored in lowercase.

### Additional details

| Field | Shown | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Authors` | Always | Author and publication year. |
| `Common name` | Always | Vernacular or common name. |
| `IUCN Red List category` | After `Show more` | IUCN Red List status. |
| `CITES status` | After `Show more` | CITES appendix status. |
| `Country conservation status` | After `Show more` | National conservation status. |
| `Sorting order` | After `Show more` | Optional numeric order for exports (such as phylogenetic order). |
| `Notes` | After `Show more` | Additional notes about the taxon. |

Fields behind `Show more` open automatically when they already contain a value.

### Manual registration

{% steps %}

1. On the project dashboard, select `Add taxon` {% ref id="G" /%} in the
   `Taxon registry` panel, then choose `Manual` {% ref id="D" /%}.
2. Select `Taxon rank` {% ref id="F" /%}.
3. Fill in the classification and optional details, then select `Add`.

{% /steps %}

### Import from file {% #import-from-file %}

{% steps %}

1. Prepare your file in `.xlsx`, `.csv`, or `.tsv`.
2. In the dashboard `Taxon registry` panel, select `Add taxon` and switch to
   `Import` {% ref id="E" /%}.
3. Select `Select file`. NAHPU maps recognized headers automatically. On narrow
   screens, switch between `Setup` and `Preview` to adjust mappings and inspect
   rows.
4. Select `Review taxa`, check the preview, select the entries to keep, and
   confirm the import. Already registered taxa are disabled and never
   overwritten.

{% /steps %}

**Delimiters and parser recovery:**

- `.csv` uses comma, `.tsv` uses tab, and `.xlsx` is read as a spreadsheet.
- For other extensions, NAHPU tries Excel, then comma, tab, and semicolon.
- Delimiter overrides and parsing details are under `Advanced options` (which
  opens automatically after a parsing error), including a custom delimiter.

{% aside type="note" %}

`.xlsx` has the best spreadsheet support; older Excel formats may fail. Re-save
as `.xlsx`, `.csv`, or `.tsv` if needed.

{% /aside %}

**Recognized columns:** `Taxon rank`, `Kingdom`, `Phylum`, `Class`, `Order`,
`Family`, `Genus`, `Specific epithet`, `Subspecific epithet`, `Authors`,
`Common name`, `IUCN Category`, `CITES status`, `Country status`,
`Sorting order`, and `Notes`. Common aliases such as `taxonClass`,
`specificEpithet`, `epithet`, `infraspecificEpithet`, `vernacularName`,
`iucnStatus`, and `appendixStatus` are also matched automatically. Unrecognized
columns default to `Ignore` and can be mapped with their dropdowns.

Valid rank values are `class`, `order`, `family`, `genus`, `species`, and
`subspecies`. Required columns depend on whether the file supplies a rank and a
known class:

| Case | Required columns | What NAHPU fills in |
| --- | --- | --- |
| No `Taxon rank` column | `Order`, `Family`, `Genus`, `Specific epithet`, and a class (mapped, or picked in `Select the class shared by all rows`) | Rank (`species`, or `subspecies` when `Subspecific epithet` is present) |
| A known class, with or without rank | Every classification column from `Class` down to the row's rank | Missing `Kingdom` and `Phylum` |
| Any other class | `Taxon rank`, `Kingdom`, `Phylum`, `Class`, and every classification column down to the rank | Nothing |

Files containing more than one class must include a `Class` column. Supplied
kingdom and phylum values are always preserved. The 14 known classes are
`Mammalia`, `Aves`, `Reptilia`, `Amphibia`, `Osteichthyes`, `Chondrichthyes`,
`Agnatha`, `Insecta`, `Arachnida`, `Chilopoda`, `Diplopoda`, `Gastropoda`,
`Bivalvia`, and `Cephalopoda`.

### Import from QR {% #import-from-qr %}

{% steps %}

1. On the source device, open `Manage taxa` (via `Taxon registry` > `Manage` or
   `Settings` > `Database` > `Taxa`), open a taxon, and tap its QR code {% ref id="B" /%}.
2. On the receiving device, choose `Add taxon` > `Import` > `Scan QR`.
3. Choose `Single taxon` to open the preview after the first valid scan, or
   `Multiple taxa` to scan a batch and select `Done` when ready to review.
4. Review the preview, select the taxa to keep, and confirm the import. Nothing
   is saved before confirmation.

{% /steps %}

- Each distinct taxon is queued once; already registered taxa cannot be
  selected and are never overwritten. Invalid or unrelated codes show an error
  while keeping the camera open.
- Cancelling leaves any previous pending import unchanged; completing a QR
  session replaces the pending file or QR preview.
- QR codes carry classification, authors, common name, and conservation
  statuses (sorting order and notes are not included). A supported rank and its
  name are sufficient; for older codes without a rank, NAHPU infers the deepest
  populated rank.
- Camera scanning works on Android, iOS, macOS, and supported web browsers. If
  camera access is denied, enable it in device settings and retry. QR image
  files and pasted payloads are not supported—use file import where camera
  scanning is unavailable.

## Edit a taxon

{% steps %}

1. Open `Manage taxa` from `Taxon registry` > `Manage` {% ref id="A" /%} on the
   dashboard (or from `Settings` > `Database` > `Taxa`).
2. Search by all fields or filter by a specific field category using the filter
   button in the search bar.
3. Open a taxon and select `Edit taxon` {% ref id="C" /%}.

{% /steps %}

## Delete taxa

In `Manage taxa`, choose `Select`, check the taxa to remove, and delete them.
Taxa used by any specimen are protected and cannot be selected (marked with an
in-use icon). Reassign those specimens to another taxon first if the entry must
be deleted.
